title: rotate silently turns an encrypted -0.0 into 0 (read side drops the sign)
updated: 2026-08-20T00:11:43Z
---

Found while measuring karr #62 (sops 3.13.3), NOT caused by it -- present before
commit a0d43c6 and after, in both formats.

File::SOPS::Encrypted::_deserialize_value returns `$data + 0.0` for a
type:float plaintext. Perl s `"-0" + 0.0` is POSITIVE zero; Go s
strconv.ParseFloat("-0", 64) is negative zero. So an encrypted negative zero
comes back out of decrypt() as +0.0 here and as -0 in sops.

The MAC does not notice: the digest is taken over decrypt_bytes ("-0"), not
over the deserialized value, so the document verifies. What drifts is the
value handed to the caller -- and therefore the document, on the next write.

Measured end to end, our own encrypt then our own rotate:

  [yaml] sops -d before rotate: negzero: -0
  [yaml] after our rotate:      negzero: 0     exit 0
  [json] sops -d before rotate: "negzero": -0
  [json] after our rotate:      "negzero": 0   exit 0

Both rotations exit 0 and report nothing. Same family as karr #63 (a rotate
changes the document silently) but a different mechanism and a much smaller
fix: the sign has to survive _deserialize_value.

Note the asymmetry that makes this worth fixing rather than documenting: the
UNENCRYPTED negative zero now round-trips correctly in both formats (karr #62),
so the encrypted slot is the only place left where File::SOPS loses the sign.

Candidate fix: recognise a leading "-" on a zero plaintext and return -0.0
(e.g. via a sign check, since 0+"-0" cannot express it). Needs a measurement
that value_to_bytes of the result is "-0" and that nothing else in the float
ladder moves. Wire lane. A regression test belongs next to
t/24-float-precision.t section 9.
DONE 2026-08-20 (commit 0359307, file-sops-wire).

_deserialize_value now restores the sign from the plaintext when the
conversion produced a zero. Both measurements the ticket asked for:

  value_to_bytes of the result is -0    yes, for all five spellings
                                        (-0, -0.0, -0.00, -0e0, -0.0e10)
  nothing else on the float ladder      33 type:float plaintexts through
  moves                                 the conversion and back out through
                                        value_to_bytes: 5 rows change, the
                                        other 28 are byte-identical

Proof, sops 3.13.3, both formats. Note the ticket's fixture assumption was
too pessimistic: sops CAN write this value when the leaf is ENCRYPTED (it
never reaches sops's float emitter), so there is a real sops -> us fixture,
unlike karr #62's unencrypted case.

  sops -e then sops -d      -0                 -0
  our decrypt               signbit 0          signbit 1
  our rotate then sops -d   0, exit 0          -0, exit 0
  our decrypt_file          0                  -0.0

One trap worth recording: the first attempt tested the converted value with
== directly. Perl's == calls SvIV_please on an integral NV and sets the
PUBLIC IOK flag in place, so a type:float plaintext of 0.0 came back as a
value detect_type calls int. The zero test now runs on a throwaway copy.
That same mechanism, from the conversion rather than from the comparison,
is now karr #73.

t/24-float-precision.t section 10; with the fix backed out it fails 3 of 24
subtests (5 assertions in each round-trip subtest, 11 of 63 in the ladder).
docs/adr/0006 amended, no new ADR.
